  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.37.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.37.0 fixes one thing that reaches this distribution directly. Renaming only the internal id of an append -- one of the three lines the engine adds inside the host's own opencode.json: the skills path, the notifier plugin, the system prompt -- while leaving the value identical made install treat it as a foreign collision, skip it, and then retire the old record: the line vanished from the document for one run and came back on the next. That matters here because a distribution is exactly where those ids move: the engine renders them from its own source, and any release that renames one would have cost its installations a run. The exclusion that keeps the engine from claiming the person's own list items did not change -- an append still has no address, because the pointer names the list and never one item -- and the new recognition by fingerprint is consulted only after both the id and the address lookups fail. An adversarial review reported that the change would let the engine adopt and later delete a hand-written line; running the same scenario against the fixed tree and against the previous one gave identical results, so that deletion is the accepted limitation of locating an append by fingerprint and not something this change introduces. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
}
